Day 236/250: Maximum Square Area by Removing Fences

Problem Statement #2975

Problem Statement Explaantion:

You are given a rectangular field of size m×n. 
There are horizontal fences at coordinates hFences and vertical fences at coordinates vFences. 
You can remove any number of existing fences.
The goal is to find the maximum possible area of a square that can be formed by the remaining fences (including the boundaries of the field at 1 and m for horizontal, and 1 and n for vertical).
Return: The maximum area modulo 10^9+7. If no square can be formed, return −1.

Solution Approach:

To form a square, we need the distance between two horizontal fences to be exactly equal to the distance between two vertical fences.

1. Identify All Fence Positions
The boundaries of the field are fixed. We must treat 1 and m as horizontal fences, and 1 and n as vertical fences.
Horizontal coordinates: [1, ..., hFences, ..., m]
Vertical coordinates: [1, ..., vFences, ..., n]

2. Calculate All Possible Gaps
A gap is the distance between any two fences. Since we can remove any fence, any pair of fences can potentially form the side of a square.
We use a HashSet to store all possible distances (h^j−h^i) between any two horizontal fences.
We then iterate through all possible distances (v^j−v^i) between any two vertical fences.

3. Find the Maximum Intersection
If a vertical gap exists in the horizontal gaps HashSet, it means a square with that side length is possible. We track the largest such side length found.

Here's the code:

import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int maximizeSquareArea(int m, int n, int[] hFences, int[] vFences) {
        // Prepare horizontal fences including boundaries
        long[] h = new long[hFences.length + 2];
        for (int i = 0; i < hFences.length; i++) h[i] = hFences[i];
        h[hFences.length] = 1;
        h[hFences.length + 1] = m;
        
        // Prepare vertical fences including boundaries
        long[] v = new long[vFences.length + 2];
        for (int i = 0; i < vFences.length; i++) v[i] = vFences[i];
        v[vFences.length] = 1;
        v[vFences.length + 1] = n;
        
        // Calculate all possible horizontal gaps
        Set<Long> hGaps = new HashSet<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < h.length; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < h.length; j++) {
                hGaps.add(Math.abs(h[i] - h[j]));
            }
        }
        
        long maxSide = -1;
        // Check all possible vertical gaps against the horizontal set
        for (int i = 0; i < v.length; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < v.length; j++) {
                long gap = Math.abs(v[i] - v[j]);
                if (hGaps.contains(gap)) {
                    maxSide = Math.max(maxSide, gap);
                }
            }
        }
        
        if (maxSide == -1) return -1;
        
        long mod = 1_000_000_007;
        return (int) ((maxSide * maxSide) % mod);
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(H2+V2)
Space Complexity:	O(H^2)

